00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.220 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome to the program  today EJ Dionne who is a columnist for  The Washington Post senior fellow at the  Brookings Institution and also author of  Code Red  how progressives and moderates can unite  to save our country so I guess that the  first question is unite to save the  country from who or what right right and  in the first instance it's obviously  trunk trumpism the attack on the rule of  law that we've been seeing a lot very  recently but all through the  administration to turn away from  democracy but it's also to save the  country from a radicalized right and  radicalized Republican party the book is  obviously a very anti-trump book but it  is also very critical of a radical right  that really now dominates the Republican  Party and is driven out all vestiges of  both moderation and back in the day even  some liberalism progressivism that  existed inside the Republican Party and  so I argue that in this circumstance  progressives and moderates and we could  argue about the term moderate I'm  careful in the book to say I know  moderate itself is a vexed term but we  could say the center left and left as  well that they have far more in common  with each other at this moment then they  then they have with anyone on the other  side their differences are much smaller  than their differences with the other  side and a subsidiary argument is that  one of the reasons arguments inside the  Democratic Party can get so fierce is  that people who are on the progressive  side have lost sort of sensible  interlocutor in the Republican Party  that almost all the major conversations  about solving public problems take place  inside the Democratic Party so Obamacare  was a program that was built in part on  ideas developed by the Heritage  Foundation and by Mitt Romney in  Massachusetts and this was cast as  somehow socialist by  the other side and so Democrats are now  having the argument that might in  another era have taken place across  party lines you expand on Obamacare or  do you go to single-payer that's the  kind of argument I make in the book but  I argue to get past this moment both of  the Trump moment end of a radicalized  conservatism progressives and moderates  need to be allied because that's the  only way out of this this morass we're  in  so does that presume this idea of vote  for the Democratic nominee no matter who  they are like is that a related idea to  what you are arguing right I think in  this circumstance there is pretty much  no alternative to that and it's obvious  that there are you know a lot of  Democrats especially after last night  who might have second thoughts about  voting for Michael Bloomberg the  probably is going to be my exact  question because you know and I think  that that will be more difficult I mean  I think it was difficult going into that  debate I think it's even more difficult  coming out of that debate similarly  there are people on the right end if you  will of this broad coalition I'm  describing who have some questions about  Bernie Sanders at this moment I think  the Bloomberg question will be the  harder question for an awful lot of  people both on the left and you know  after the demolition job that Elizabeth  Warren did particularly among  progressive women yeah and you know I've  said I'm not supporting Bloomberg in the  primary but if it's November 3rd and  it's Bloomberg versus Trump I'm voting  for Bloomberg and I've pointed out I  think on Supreme Court justice  selections Bloomberg will make far  better choices than what Trump has  evidenced he would do I've listed a few  things and it doesn't mean I'm any great  fan of him but I know a lot of people in  my audience don't feel that way at all  and might my sort of follow-on question  to this is I believe in and support what  we would call a vigorous debate during  the primary about  which ideas should reign supreme so to  speak but I wonder is there a point at  which it becomes self-destructive in the  eventual general election yeah I am  worried about that I may have noticed I  have a column up today where and this  was here I wrote most of it before the  debate that the kind of demolition-derby  that Democrats are having is leading to  their ignoring right on that stage last  night  what is going on outside it was really  striking how little Trump himself  was mentioned we are really at a  potential constitutional crisis here  with what's going on inside the Justice  Department what has gone on with Roger  stone how all these cases are being  cherry picked on Ukraine and Russia by  by bar these are very disturbing things  it did disturb me that Democrats were  spending so much time sort of settling  their own differences that they didn't  even get to hey we're all here because  we need to put a stop to this you know  on your Bloomberg point I think you're  correct that court appointments on what  his budgets would look like even his tax  program is far more progressive than  anything Republicans had to offer and  his support for both the climate  movements and especially the gun  movements is a real thing he's made a  huge difference there I think that what  we saw last night will raise questions  not only on the left but a lot of other  people saying is this AG is he actually  the strongest candidate to put against  Trump I think that was the argument he  needed to make and clearly last night on  that front was a fail no certainly if  his making billions depended on winning  debates I don't know that he would be  nearly nearly as wealthy as he is was my  reality yeah so now and in terms of when  we've seen in the past elections that  are heavily based around defeating the  incumbent so John Kerry in 2004 against  George W Bush strikes me  one of those there is often the argument  made that you've really got to be voting  for something as opposed to trying to  remove the incumbent that it often  doesn't go well when the main messages  remove the incumbent so do you agree  with that and is that maybe something  that tempers the problem of Trump not  being mentioned that much yesterday  which in a way I found refreshing to  just hear the candidates talk about  something other than Trump well I think  there are two sides to this and maybe we  can take Ronald Reagan as the example  one of my arguments in the book by the  way is that a key reason why there has  been a resurgence of democratic  socialism in the u.s. which by the way  has a long American tradition as I point  out in the book Eugene Debs got 6% of  the boat there were actually 1,200 local  elected officials who were socialists  back in that election the reason why  you've had a revival of democratic  socialism is a reaction to the dominance  of our public conversation by the Reagan  economic consensus and so the rise of  democratic socialism is a reaction  against that but what did Reagan do to  create that long hegemony that long  period of conservative dominance I think  there were two sides of it I think  running against an incumbent who's  unpopular is actually a helpful thing  politically Jimmy Carter was unpopular  and I argue in the book for what I call  the power of negative thinking that when  you looked at what Ronald Reagan built  his program on it started out as a  series of negatives anti-tax  anti-communist anti-government I think  what pulls together this coalition are a  similar set of antes obviously anti  Trump and all the things that represents  you know a the oppression of immigrants  and refugees a you know corporate  corruption cast as populism a host it's  disrespect for within a whole series of  things but also a backlash against a  Republican Party that is simply given up  on public  problem-solving on using government to  address inequality to provide health  insurance a whole series of areas where  they proposed simply retreating cutting  taxes relying entirely on the public  sector but out of that negative comes a  series of positives and if I can just  pick three quick issues that are really  burning issues that raise real  coalition-building possibilities right  now the entire Democratic Party the  entire spectrum from Saturday left  now wants to provide affordable good  health insurance to every American  that's not even debated anymore the  debate is over whether we do it through  a public option and building on  Obamacare or go directly up to  single-payer lord I do not think we  should divide ourselves radically over  the path we could disagree we can debate  the path but we all want to go broadly  in the same direction aoc underscored  that recently with their comments second  one is free college that's not so  radical we used to have it before the  massive under investment in public  education whether we get there in one  step or many steps and let's make sure  this includes not only college but also  training for people who don't want to go  to college in the book I quote Richard  Cordray ran for governor of Ohio  unfortunately he didn't win but I love  this line he said you shouldn't have to  go to college to join the middle class I  think we need to broaden that  conversation but that's got a lot of  possibility and lastly the green new  deal which when you boil the green New  Deal down it is saying that we need  urgently to act on the climate and we  also urgently need to restructure the  economy to make it fairer and we need to  do that partly to address losses that  some groups might suffer from taking  bold action on the climate I think these  are all positives that grow out of all  of those negatives so I guess I am not  worried that a pure antitrump campaign  will lack a positive vision of  because I think you're hearing a lot of  positives in all these in the mess of  all these debates there were actually a  lot of proposals well the book I want to  make clear is not proposing this vague  idea of unity for the sake of unity the  way that we've seen criticized over the  last couple of months as we've seen the  Democratic primary shift it's really a  practical and a sensible perspective on  what it is simply that needs to happen  the book is by EJ Dionne it is called  code read how progressives and moderates  can unite to save our country EJ so  great having you on I appreciate your  time thank you so much I hope we can do  this again

